WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
A common Go validation failure is trusting client-side checks.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:11.160
Browser validation, frontend TypeScript, or mobile client rules can improve usability, but they are not the authority.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:18.300
The server must validate because requests can be sent directly, clients can be modified, and old versions can remain in use.

4
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:26.880
Another common issue is accepting unknown JSON fields unintentionally, which can hide caller mistakes and make API contracts harder to reason about.

5
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.000
Zero values are another recurring source of bugs.

6
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:41.980
If a missing field and an intentionally provided zero both become the same Go value, the handler may approve a request that should have failed or overwrite data unintentionally.

7
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.120
Pointers, custom request types, explicit presence tracking, and careful validation can clarify the difference.

8
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.940
This is especially important in patch-style endpoints and partial updates.

9
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.620
Validation should not be mistaken for authorization or safe API use.

10
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.680
A valid user ID still needs an access check.

11
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.380
A validated search string should still be used with parameterized queries rather than string-built SQL.

12
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:13.080
A validated command option should still avoid unsafe process execution patterns.

13
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.340
A cleaned path should still be checked against allowed directories.

14
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:23.040
Validation reduces risk, but it is one layer in a larger defensive design.

15
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:31.000
Mass assignment style risks can appear in Go when request structs are copied too broadly into persistence models or update maps.

16
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:39.820
Clients should not be able to set server-controlled fields such as role, owner ID, tenant ID, approval state, or audit values.

17
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:47.120
Business logic validation gaps can be even subtler: the input has the right shape, but the requested state change is not allowed.

18
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.900
Strong Go services validate both the data and the operation.
